{
  "description": "SASRec predicts the next interaction using causal self-attention over a user's item history. Item and position embeddings pass through attention and feed-forward blocks; the resulting features score candidate items through shared item embeddings. Training uses positive next items and sampled negatives. Source: [Self-Attentive Sequential Recommendation](https://arxiv.org/html/1808.09781v1).",
  "motivation": "Attention can emphasize recent interactions in sparse histories while retaining access to older relevant actions. The supplied task establishes SASRec as the starting baseline; it does not specify a new cold-start mechanism. ID-based SASRec alone cannot learn useful representations for items absent from training.",
  "implementation_notes": "Retain RecHarness sasrec_perdataset and routing for all four categories. Use preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, DATA_DIR=os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], and the environment's SASREC_EPOCHS default of 200. Keep splits and host evaluation unchanged; select using macro validation HR@10. Store artifacts beneath the trial directory and keep predictor generation consistent with trained checkpoints. Preserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and the supplied compatibility contract. Any proposed content extension must use only permitted public metadata and requires validation; no benchmark improvement is established here.",
  "pseudocode": "For each category:\n  Load unchanged RecHarness splits and item mapping.\n  Initialize SASRec with seed 42.\n  For each training batch:\n    Embed padded item sequences and positions.\n    Apply causal attention and feed-forward blocks.\n    Score positive next items and sampled negatives.\n    Minimize binary cross-entropy over valid positions.\n  Save checkpoints and architecture configuration beneath the trial directory.\nGenerate working/predict.py with matching architecture and category routing.\nUse the pinned host validation protocol to compare trials by macro HR@10.",
  "originality": {
    "score": 2,
    "positive": "The original paper introduced an influential attention-based approach to sequential recommendation.",
    "negative": "Relative to the required RecHarness baseline, the supplied idea adds no concrete architectural or training innovation. Scores use a 1–10 scale."
  },
  "future_potential": {
    "score": 6,
    "positive": "Permitted titles and descriptions could support a separately validated extension for sparse item histories.",
    "negative": "Such an extension is unspecified, and its benefit under the fixed evaluation and search budget is unknown."
  },
  "code_difficulty": {
    "score": 3,
    "positive": "Starting from the existing SASRec implementation makes baseline integration relatively straightforward; higher scores indicate greater difficulty.",
    "negative": "Architecture changes require synchronized checkpoint loading, predictor generation, dataset routing, and diagnostic adapters."
  }
}
